meta {
  name: Submit Form
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/v1/clients/{{clientId}}/forms/{{formId}}/submissions
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-API-Key: {{formKey}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "fields": {
      "firstName": "Jane",
      "lastName": "Doe",
      "email": "jane@example.com",
      "interestedIn": "Website redesign",
      "budget": "$10k-$25k",
      "comment": "Sent from Bruno."
    }
  }
}

docs {
  The Form 01 scenario (sol-brain/sol-api/03-scenario-form-01.md). Returns
  202 with a `submissionId`; integrations and notifications then run in a
  Workflow (`sol-gate-submission*`), so check its instance — or the
  `submission processed` log line — for the outcome.

  `formKey` is a key of this form (`sgk_…`): create one with sol-api's
  Forms → Create Form API Key, against the same environment. Point `clientId`
  / `formId` at a form whose `payload_schema` these fields satisfy, or edit
  them. Only `fields` is read — anything else in the body is ignored.

  Each call does real work (Mailchimp write, emails) and counts toward the
  form's rate limit (60/min).
}
